CONF68K known divergencesTest IDs listed here are disagreements with DOCS/claims.md that havealready been investigated and characterised. The runner reports them asKNOWN rather than as new findings, so that a familiar defect does notdrown out a fresh one. A line is "<test-id> <one-line explanation>".An empty list is the intended steady state: it means every claim inclaims.md holds on every system we can currently reach. It is no longerempty. Everything below was found by the record-locking tests on theirfirst run against an RBF image, and every one of them is os9exec's, notOS-9's -- they are recorded here so that a fix shows up as CHANGED, whichis news, instead of as silence.Note that both remaining findings are RBF-image ones. On a host-native directorythese tests report SKIP and prove nothing, which is why DOCS/expected andDOCS/expected-rbf are separate files.t25 EOF lock does not hold against another WRITER. The manual gives the    lock's purpose as avoiding "problems that could occur when two users    try to simultaneously extend a file", so a second process writing at    the end is the case it exists for; here that write succeeds. The lock    is not absent -- it is consulted on the read path, which is the half    that makes the documented spooler-behind-assembler behaviour work, so    the visible symptom is narrow: readers cannot pass the writer, and    writers can.t27 The single-user bit in an I$Open access mode is not enforced. I$Open    documents bit 6 as "open file for non sharable use" and page 7-8 says    a second open must return error 253; both opens succeed here. Unlike    the three above this involves no blocking at all, so it is the one    locking claim that would still report a real verdict on a system where    a blocked process is never resumed.